From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Chad Cormier Roussel <chadcormierroussel@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 22:03:10 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] d3d12: Let every timestamp ride an encoder through one
 counter buffer.

Apple GPUs write one counter buffer per encoder, and each timestamp query
heap had its own, so a timestamp could only ride an encoder sampling its
heap's buffer: the others, and all but the last of several waiting for
one encoder's start, took a blit encoder of their own (70 a frame in a
SMITE 2 match, against D3DMetal's 3).

Each queue now samples every timestamp into one counter buffer, a ring
of 4096 slots (Metal allows 32 counter buffers per process, and each
timestamp heap still holds one). An encoder takes, in one attachment,
the timestamps waiting before it at its start and its own at its end,
whatever their heaps; timestamps at one point share one sample. Those
before a clear or resolve pass (which wait before their fragment stage)
or at the end of the call ride the previous encoder's end; those before
the MetalFX upscale ride its begin blit. A blit of their own is left
only where no encoder in the call can take them. Once the command buffer
completes, the queue writes each slot's value to its queries' values in
their heap, and ResolveQueryData's CPU resolves copy those (aliases as
before).

DXMT_D3D12_TIMESTAMP_BLITS=1, or no counter buffer left for the ring,
samples into the heaps' own buffers as before.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
